ning & Meal Plans Living Learning Communities Apply for Housing Living on Campus Commuter Resources Campus Activities Campus Events SA/LD Clubs & Organizations Campus Recreation Events in New Britain Student Services Veterans Affairs Blue Chip Card Office Student Center Office of Career & Professional Development Support Services Support Services Central is committed to providing a safe and healthy campus environment with numerous co-curricular opportunities, programs, services, and activities to enhance the student experience. Support Services Health & Wellness Health Services Counseling Wellness Education Victim Advocacy Campus Police Title IX Centers & Initiatives The Ruthe Boyea Women's Center LGBTQ Center Brotherhood Initiative Campus Faith Consultants Office of Veteran's Affairs Family Resources Academic Support Academic Advising The Learning Center Writing Center Accessibility Services Success Central We Are Central We Are Central Central Connecticut State University is a community of learners dedicated to teaching, research, and outreach. We prepare students to be thoughtful, responsible, and successful citizens. About Central University Information About Central Location, Maps, & Directions Age Friendly University Accreditation Fast Facts Consumer Information & Disclosures Organization President Zulma R. Toro Leadership Team Academic Affairs Academic Schools Exploring a Research 2 Polytechnic University Designation Experiential Learning Innovative Spaces Sustainability & Energy Search Search News September 4, 2026 Study Abroad group navigates Norway September 2, 2026 Film screening featured in New Britain Progressive August 28, 2026 Grant award to foster job-readiness on a new SCALE Events September 8 Beta Alpha Psi School Supplies Drive September 9 Beta Alpha Psi School Supplies Drive September 12 Forum: What does 9/11 mean, 25 years later? University Calendars Blackboard Central Email Campus Map Academic Programs Faculty/Staff Directory IT Services News Library Grant award to foster job-readiness on a new SCALE Published: August 28, 2026 Central was awarded a Connecticut Tech Talent Accelerator Innovation Grant for $75,000 earlier this month that will address a growing workforce gap: Employees who not only know how to find information, but also how to determine what information can be trusted and how to explain that to people who might not be experts in a given field. The grant, awarded by the Business-Higher Education Forum and the New England Board of Higher Education, with support from the Connecticut Office of Workforce Strategy, will support the development of a new three-credit course, Translational Literacy in Science. Led by principal investigator Dr. Caleb Bragg, the course will teach students to evaluate evidence across disciplines and translate complex technical information for non-specialist audiences through Scientific Communication + AI Literacy Experience (SCALE) training. SCALE will teach students to scrutinize information from vendors, clinical summaries, and AI-generated documents and trace the claims back to primary sources. From there, they will be able to distinguish what the evidence supports, identify what has been missed or overstated, and communicate remaining uncertainties. Bragg notes, “Information has never been easier to get, or harder to evaluate. Teaching students to do both is what SCALE is for.” The grant also will support paid micro-internships that give students an opportunity to apply those skills to real projects for Connecticut employers. The placements will be mentored learning experiences in coordination with the Central Workforce and Innovation Hub, with employers giving feedback on the students' assessments and communication. Central will serve as the employer of record, handling payroll, onboarding, and timesheets. The micro-internship program is currently recruiting employer partners. In the spring, up to 40 students will take the three-credit Translational Literacy in Science course. An employer convening in April will define projects and match them with students, followed by paid micro-internships of 20 to 25 hours during the summer of 2027. For employers, the program provides an opportunity to work with students who have been trained in evidence evaluation and communication while getting an early look at potential future employees. Students will acquire job skills that will be steadily more applicable across disciplines and workplaces. “For years, students have asked me some version of the same question: How do I know if this is true? Even my best graduates, the ones headed for PhD programs, asked it after I had taught them everything I know about how psychological science verifies truth,” Bragg says. “Not everything out there comes with a significance test attached. Good decisions rest on what counts as reliable evidence in that particular area and on being able to explain that to someone standing outside it.” 1615 Stanley Street CT 06050 Directions 860.832.3200 Central Facebook Central Twitter Central Instagram Central Linkedin Central YouTube Contact Us Campus Map Athletics Campus Safety Wellness Services Title IX Search Search Main Navigation (Mobile) Home Admissions First-Year Students Transfer Students Graduate Students International Students Adult/Returning Students Accepted Students Visit Central Tuition & Aid Academics Programs & Majors Departments Schools Register for Classes Calendar Student Support Services Student Life Student Affairs Residence Life Dining & Meal Plans Campus Events Student Activities & Leadership Development About Utility Navigation Apply Visit Give Athletics Calendar Blackboard Central Email Campus Map Courses & Programs Faculty/Staff Directory IT Services News Library"
    },
